# without its tests, testdata, testing/ and http/e2e/ packages, docs, agent
# guides, or dev and CI files. scripts/update-coraza.sh copies a release or
# a commit on main the same way each time and applies scripts/coraza/*.patch
# in name order: upstream PRs not merged yet and our own fixes. A patch
# upstream took no longer applies and is deleted.
# govulncheck sees this copy without a version, so Coraza's advisories
# (github.com/corazawaf/coraza/security/advisories) are checked by hand.
#
# Changes, each marked "Changed by Ostiole" in the source:
# - internal/bodyprocessors/multipart.go: parts were read with NextPart,
#   which decodes a part sent with Content-Transfer-Encoding
#   quoted-printable and hides that header. Backends are handed the raw
#   bytes, so a payload written with =XX escapes or soft line breaks got
#   past rules that match the raw form. Parts are read with NextRawPart.
#   Upstream: PR #1759 (2026-10-14), open at 08aa511.
# - internal/collections, internal/bodyprocessors, internal/cookies,
#   internal/url, internal/corazawaf and http: collections were Go maps, so
#   ARGS, cookies, headers and their _NAMES came out in a new order on every
#   evaluation, and a chain reading its parent's last match (TX:0-9,
#   MATCHED_VAR) could deny a request on one transaction and pass it on the
#   next (CRS 942130, 942131, 942521). A Map keeps its keys in insertion
#   order, and the parsers add in input order. coraza-caddy feeds headers
#   itself, from a map, so header order under it is still unsorted.
#   Upstream: issue #1815 and PR #1834 (2026-10-26), open at 08aa511.
# - internal/actions/ctl.go, internal/corazawaf/rulegroup.go, transaction.go
#   and waf.go, internal/seclang/directives.go: ctl:ruleRemoveTargetById,
#   ByTag and ByMsg, and ctl:ruleRemoveByTag and ByMsg, walked every rule by
#   value on every request, and a target removal then added an entry per
#   selected rule, one allocation each. ctl:ruleRemoveTargetByTag=OWASP_CRS
#   selects every CRS rule, which cost 68 us and 720 allocations a request. A
#   ctl caches the IDs it selects per version of the rule group, which every
#   added, removed or in-place edited rule changes, and the removals share one
#   capped slice: 24 us and 118 allocations. Rules without an id are no longer
#   touched by ctl removals: all share ID 0, so one removal hit them all and
#   skipped every SecMarker (the ctl part of upstream PR #1782).
#   Upstream: issue #1848 and PR #1849 (2026-11-08), open at 08aa511.
